wind-speed-answer-why = Air cannot be seen, so we watch something that moves with it. A soap bubble is a thin film with air inside; it weighs next to nothing, and the wind at once carries it at its own speed. Then v = l / t: 6 m divided by the time between the marks. Meteorologists measure the wind’s speed too — with anemometers — and sailors long judged it by its signs: the Beaufort scale (1805) matches each force of wind with what it does. When leaves rustle and the wind is felt on the face, that is force 2 — 1.6 to 3.4 m/s. Timing by hand has an error of about 0.2 s, so for a stronger wind a longer distance is better, and several bubbles: the wind comes in gusts, too.
wind-speed-answer-say = Leaves rustle — the air moves at { $leaves }: force 2, a light breeze.
wind-speed-answer-text = {" "}The wind’s force by its speed matched its signs (the Beaufort scale).
